{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261125T230216Z-7af05777", "ts": "2026-11-25T23:06:24Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/tripwire-disabled", "build": "bb1fbd856e2c"}, "body": {"base_commit": "bb1fbd856e2cd7ca23acbc2d59a801da95da06bd", "base_tree": "94384f63265ff24de1d90cfbc47c58aba0044540", "head_tree": "7d41203cfb77468caa0d15f8b431c187fdb7e46f", "head_commit": "83f262b6949ac44170d9492f1ca418ba26ae5537", "dispatch_commit": "83f262b6949ac44170d9492f1ca418ba26ae5537", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_plugin_activation.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/changes/tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "tests/test_plugin_activation.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/changes/tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The agreement test that holds the inline verdict to disabled_by skips managed drop-ins and unreadable files, which leaves those two branches of the home untested", "recommendation": "test_disabled_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation is the one guard behind the 'every fact has one home' disposition ('a test holds it to the home'). The change record says it covers 'every precedence case'. Its parametrize list has no managed-settings.d drop-in and no unreadable or non-dict settings file. Those are exactly the branches where the two copies are written differently: the home uses Path.glob plus sorted Path objects and read_text(encoding='utf-8') with (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError, ValueError); the inline copy uses os.listdir with endswith('.json') plus sorted strings and open() with (IOError, OSError, ValueError). Neither branch has a test in the home either. test_disabled_by_takes_the_highest_source_naming_the_plugin never creates managed-settings.d, and only the shell-run tests (inline copy) cover the unreadable file and the drop-in. Consequence: a later edit to either copy's drop-in ordering or unreadable-file handling stays green while the tripwire and check-plugin-active disagree, which is the drift the inline-copy exception was granted on condition of preventing. Fix: add to the agreement parametrization a drop-in case (managed-settings.d/10-x.json false, with and without a managed-settings.json naming the plugin) and an unreadable higher-precedence file over a lower false. That one list then covers both branches in both copies. Per core.md, 'A coverage, completeness or absence claim (\u2018X now handles Y\u2019, \u2018no Y\u2019, \u2018all\u2019) holds only when its falsifying query returns nothing', and 'every precedence case' fails that test today. Scope: instance, the single parametrize list.", "files": ["tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_plugin_activation.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A settings-resolution branch handled differently by plugin_activation.disabled_by and the inline _VERDICT_PY, and absent from the agreement test's case list.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py::test_disabled_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation (no managed-settings.d case)"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py::test_disabled_verdict_agrees_with_plugin_activation (no unreadable or non-dict settings-file case)"}], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The new `disabled` status is routed in each skill's status table but not in the instructions that act on that status later in the same skill", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: the change added a row to each routing table, but later steps in the same skills enumerate the statuses again by name (`inactive` or `unknown`), so `disabled` falls through at each of them. In onboard, the table says `disabled` \u2192 'Do not report success'. Finish step 2, however, lists what the report must raise as 'the plugin check was `inactive` or `unknown`', and its 'Lead with the outcome \u2014 onboarded' then reads as success. Step 3's 'anywhere else' row ends on 'open the target ... start discovery there', with a fix step only 'when the check said `inactive`'. An owner with a disabled target is therefore sent to a session that loads nothing, with no re-enable step. The in-target row likewise names only `inactive`/`unknown`. In migrate, step 3's new row defers to step 8 'on the same terms as `inactive`', but step 8 gives wording only for `inactive` (run the install line, which is the wrong fix here) and `unknown`. In doctor, the Classify section's **broken** list (remedy: `/prawduct:migrate` or install) omits #19's `disabled`, though #19 now rates it broken. Fix it by construction, not by adding a fifth name to each list: phrase each downstream site relationally, as 'any status but `active`, relaying the command's own text, which names the fix'. A future status then inherits the routing. Name `disabled` only where its fix differs, which is doctor's broken-class remedy.", "files": ["plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md"], "scope": "class", "closure": "A skill instruction outside a check-plugin-active routing table that branches on, or lists, that command's statuses by name.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md Finish step 2 report bullet 'the plugin check was `inactive` or `unknown`'"}, {"id": "m2", "site": "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md Finish step 3 'in the target' row ('after relaying an `inactive` or `unknown` check result')"}, {"id": "m3", "site": "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md Finish step 3 'anywhere else' row ('the install command comes first when the check said `inactive`')"}, {"id": "m4", "site": "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md step 8 (wording given for `inactive` and `unknown` only)"}, {"id": "m5", "site": "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md 'Classify and report' broken-class definition (omits #19 `disabled`)"}], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The tripwire's DISABLED_MESSAGE tells the agent to branch on a fact it cannot know at session start", "recommendation": "Framework Check 8 (Instruction Clarity). Scope: instance. The line the tripwire injects says: 'If the user switched it off here on purpose (/prawduct:repo-disable), work without it; otherwise tell them before any work.' When the hook fires, the agent has no evidence either way. The only evidence it does have, an explicit `false` in a settings file, reads like intent. Two reasonable agents therefore diverge. One assumes the opt-out was deliberate and works in silence, which is the exact outcome #1246 exists to end. The other interrupts before every task in a repo the owner disabled on purpose. The plan lists this tone as an open, builder-inferred decision for the boundary review. Make the branch decidable from what the agent can observe. For example: always tell the user once, in one line naming the file, then work without governance unless they say the setting is a mistake. Or branch on the file: `settings.local.json` is a personal opt-out, so mention it; the committed `settings.json` of a repo with `.prawduct/` is a contradiction, so tell them before any work. Keep `tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py`'s `/prawduct:repo-disable` assertion pinned to the result.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "The one LLM-facing line the tripwire prints for a `disabled:` verdict.", "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "DISABLED_MESSAGE drops the --plugin-dir escape that the inactive line and the change's own doctor wording both name", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the tone decision weighed only the deliberate /prawduct:repo-disable case. It missed the case the change itself identifies. report_lines' DOCTOR/MIGRATE branch for `disabled` says the plugin can be present while the setting is false because it 'was loaded another way (`--plugin-dir`)'. INACTIVE_MESSAGE ends with '(Loaded the plugin with --plugin-dir? Then ignore this line.)' for the same reason. DISABLED_MESSAGE has no such escape. Example: a developer disables the marketplace copy in a governed repo and runs a dev copy with --plugin-dir. Every session then injects 'PRAWDUCT GOVERNANCE IS SWITCHED OFF IN THIS SESSION ... work without it' next to a loaded briefing, and the agent may skip governance that is in fact live. Fix: give DISABLED_MESSAGE the same --plugin-dir clause INACTIVE_MESSAGE carries, and pin it in test_a_project_setting_of_false_is_the_disabled_line_naming_the_file. Because the tripwire text changes, the consumer note's install-tripwire --apply instruction already covers the rollout.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "scope": "instance", "closure": "A tripwire message that claims governance is absent without the --plugin-dir caveat the code elsewhere admits can apply.", "members": [{"id": "m1", "site": "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py DISABLED_MESSAGE"}], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "record-lint governed-by-gap: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md:23", "recommendation": "api-contract carries 3 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 2 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md"], "source": "record-lint", "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Drop-in precedence order is an unverified model of Claude Code's managed-settings merge", "recommendation": "settings_sources and the inline verdict rank managed-settings.json above its managed-settings.d drop-ins, and rank drop-ins in ascending name order, with the first file naming the plugin deciding. The source the plan cites (plugins/loading, 'find where a plugin is enabled') is about scope precedence, and the plan marks the drop-in order as 'inferred, mine'. Claude Code's settings documentation describes drop-ins as following the systemd convention: the base file is merged first and drop-ins are merged on top in name order, so later files win. If that is current, the order here is inverted. A drop-in setting false over a base file setting true would then read as active, which is the silent case #1246 closes, and the reverse case would name the wrong file. I could not settle this from the installed binary, so it stays an observation. Check it against the current settings docs before the owner confirms the inferred decision. test_managed_settings_outrank_the_project pins the current order and would need to change with it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Tests now read the machine's real managed-settings directories", "recommendation": "TRIPWIRE_COMMAND hard-codes MANAGED_SETTINGS_DIRS (/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode, /etc/claude-code). plugin_activation_status defaults to them, and so does the CLI. Every _run_hook test, test_cli_exits_1_when_disabled, and every pre-existing plugin_activation_status call without managed_dirs=() therefore consults host-global files. On a machine whose organisation policy names prawduct@prawduct in enabledPlugins, those tests change outcome for reasons unrelated to the code. That breaks the test-determinism page's 'the same on any machine' premise, though it matches none of P1-P8. Such a machine is rare today, so this is not a finding.", "files": ["tests/test_unloaded_tripwire.py", "tests/test_plugin_activation.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "Inline verdict opens settings files with the locale encoding; the home reads UTF-8", "recommendation": "en() calls json.load(open(s)), while disabled_by uses read_text(encoding='utf-8'). On Python 3.0-3.6 under a C/POSIX locale, a settings file with non-ASCII content fails to decode in the hook, is passed over, and the two verdicts can diverge. The hook's stated floor is 3.0. The impact is narrow and degrades toward silence, not toward accusation.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Descriptions of the tripwire still say it fires only on a missing install record", "recommendation": "Per the learnings rule that opens 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees', several subject-file sentences now describe the trigger incompletely. onboard SKILL.md 'Either way' says the tripwire 'prints a warning into the session when the install record has no entry for the repo (nothing when it has one)'. migrate SKILL.md's summary says it 'warns when the plugin is not installed for the repo'. migrate step 3 says 'the install record decides what loads next session'. Each is incomplete rather than misleading, since the routing tables and doctor #1 carry the new case, so no fix commit is owed for them alone. If m1-m5 are edited anyway, make these sentences relational ('when the plugin will not load here') rather than adding a second clause.", "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "MANAGED_SETTINGS_DIRS reads both platforms' managed directories on every OS", "recommendation": "`plugin_activation.MANAGED_SETTINGS_DIRS` and the committed hook text pass both `/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode` and `/etc/claude-code` everywhere. Claude Code reads only its own platform's directory. A stray `/etc/claude-code/managed-settings.json` on macOS would therefore read as a managed `false` that outranks everything: a false 'switched off' line, against the module's stated stance of never claiming governance is off on a guess. Windows' managed location is not read at all, which fails soft, so it is fine. Rare enough to leave unfixed. If it is ever touched, select the directory by platform in the one home (`sys.platform`), and with `uname` in the shell half.", "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "repo-disable's 'what happens next' does not mention the new session-start line", "recommendation": "plugin/skills/repo-disable/SKILL.md step 4 says the override turns off 'commands, hooks, and banner'. In an onboarded repo the committed tripwire now prints DISABLED_MESSAGE every session, and that message says to tell the user 'otherwise'. The agent cannot tell a deliberate opt-out from an accidental one, so in practice it will mention it every session. This is the intended design, but the disabling user is never told it will happen. One added sentence in step 4 would close it. Not rated a finding because nobody takes a wrong action: the line names repo-disable itself.", "files": ["plugin/skills/repo-disable/SKILL.md", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The architecture decision that licenses the tripwire's inline copy names only the install-record rule", "recommendation": "architecture.md's 2026-10-27 unloaded-tripwire DECISION justifies 'a self-contained copy of plugin_activation's install-record rule' in the product repo. This change widens that copy: the enabledPlugins precedence walk, plus the managed-settings directories passed as arguments. The reason is recorded in the build plan's 'every fact has one home' disposition and in the change fragment, but not in the decision a future reader of the norm will open. A dated line under that decision, without amending the clause, would keep the departure's scope visible there.", "files": ["plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Three builder-inferred decisions await the owner at this boundary", "recommendation": "The plan's Goals list three decisions as 'inferred, mine, open for the owner at the boundary review': the disabled tone, exit 1 shared with inactive, and the precedence edge cases. This is that boundary, and a reviewer cannot confirm them. Surface them to the owner for confirm-or-correct before merge.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py", "plugin/lib/unloaded_tripwire.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Records Pass: covered set and result", "recommendation": "Covered all four files_oracle entries: api-contract.md (the check-plugin-active paragraph), build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md, changes/tripwire-disabled.md and owner-rulings.md (the 2026-11-25 entry). None clears a bar. The change fragment's claims (precedence order, only literal false disables, unreadable files passed over, --settings not consulted, exit 1, the stale-until-install-tripwire --apply consumer note) match the code. Its 'a test holds the two to the same verdicts over every precedence case' is slightly broad: the agreement test does not cover managed drop-ins or an unreadable file, which other tests cover. It is inert, and no edit is wanted. The ruling's reference to build-plan-handoff-notes-persist.md points to a plan that exists only on fix/handoff-notes-persist. That is an append-only ruling record, so it is not a finding. The api-contract governed-by-gap is record-lint's and is not restated here.", "oid": "O-9"}], "duration_seconds": 540, "scope": "tripwire-disabled", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01"], "chunks_proposed": ["01"], "chunks_declared": false, "chunks_evidence": {"01": {"paths": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/plugin_activation.py"], "paths_more": 3, "commits": ["7a903d58b90f"]}}, "base_reviewed": "bb1fbd856e2cd7ca23acbc2d59a801da95da06bd", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/changes/tripwire-disabled.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/skills/doctor/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/migrate/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/onboard/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md", "findings": [{"check": "governed-by-gap", "path": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-tripwire-disabled.md", "line": 23, "detail": "api-contract carries 3 `## Direction` norm(s) but this plan disposes of 2 \u2014 each norm needs a recorded disposition, and \"inapplicable, because \u2014\" is one"}], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 1, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dead": 0, "change-fragment": 0, "retired-claim-survives": 0, "late-requirement-record": 0, "test-scope-conftest": 0, "test-scope-autouse": 0, "test-scope-init": 0, "test-clock-fake": 0, "test-clock-import": 0}}, "span": "full", "bounded_from": null, "span_reason": "no-boundary-review", "dispatched_at": "2026-11-